@version 0.01

@h1[center]{
Cantilever Beam Deflection}

@p{
A cantilever beam is fixed at the wall on the left and free at the right end, where a point load P is applied. This example computes the beam's deflection curve in closed form and renders it as an interactive diagram: drag the load slider below the diagram to see the curve update live.}

@h2{
Section Properties}

@p{
Span, modulus of elasticity, and moment of inertia, in consistent
inch-kip units.}

L := 120;     // span, in
E := 29000;   // modulus of elasticity, ksi (A992 steel)
I := 800;     // moment of inertia, in^4

@h2{
Load}

P := 12;      // applied load, kip  // @input slider min=1 max=40 step=1 target="deflection,def_{max}"
P_{max} := 40;  // slider's max value, used only to fix the plot's vertical scale

@h2{
Deflection Formula}

@p{
For a cantilever beam with a point load P at the free end, the deflection at any point x along the span (measured from the wall) is given below. It is defined once as a callable formula, so the same expression can be evaluated both along the live curve (at the current P) and at the fixed worst case P_max used to scale the plot.}

deflection(P, x) := -P*x^2*(3*L-x)/(6*E*I);

@hidden
d_max = deflection(P_{max}, L);

@h2{
Deflection Diagram}

@p{
The diagram below shows the beam fixed at the wall (left, hatched) with load P applied at the free end (right, arrow). The blue curve is the beam's deflected shape.}

svg(400, 175,
    line(10,55, 10,125),
    line(10,55, 0,65),
    line(10,71, 0,81),
    line(10,87, 0,97),
    line(10,103, 0,113),
    line(10,119, 0,129),
    line(10,90, 390,90),

    arrow(390,15, 390,85),
    text(385,10, "P"),
    curve(deflection(P, x), x, 0, L, 40, "deflection", d_max)
);
// curve()'s 7th arg fixes the vertical scale to the worst-case deflection (at
// P_max) so the curve visibly grows as P is dragged up, not auto-rescaled.

@h2{
Result}

@result
def_{max} := deflection(P, L);
